  <!-- ══ THE PAYLOAD CONFIG SPLIT (2026-09-10, DECISIONS ruling, lane customer-update-path) ══════
       Adrian's ruling: "the customer update path is a SHIPPED SCRIPT plus a HARMLESS ZIP, not an
       in-app installer." Harmless means the artefact cannot destroy customer state HOWEVER it is
       extracted - including the way it had always actually been used, which is an operator copying
       the zip over their install directory. Procedure stops being the thing that protects them.

       Every file below is in exactly one of two buckets, and the bucket is the answer to one
       question: IS AN OPERATOR EXPECTED TO EDIT THIS, AND WOULD THEIR EDIT BE LOST?

         config.default/  OPERATOR-EDITABLE. Shipped as a default; the real file is created in
                          config\ on first run by Data\Services\ConfigDefaultsSeeder, per file, only
                          when absent. No upgrade route writes over it, because no upgrade route
                          writes to config\ at all. installer\SQLTriage.iss ships these
                          'onlyifdoesntexist' for the same reason and preserves them across the
                          pre-upgrade uninstall (OperatorConfigNames there is the same set).

         config/          BUILD OR PRODUCT ARTEFACT. Not operator state; MUST be refreshed by every
                          upgrade. Shipping these first-run-only would be a NEW defect: a stale
                          version.json is exactly the 2026-08-03 failure where the binary was new and
                          the service reported the old build to AutoUpdateService and
                          AzureBlobExportService alike.

