       install overwrites ONLY config\version.json, config\free-bundle.dat,
       config\health-benchmark.json and config\schemas\ - four files nobody authored. It cannot reach
       .seat-register-key, .sqlite-cipher-key, portal-settings.json or server-connections.json at all,
       because those have never been in the payload. If a file is ever moved between these two groups
       and that property stops holding, the move is wrong. PayloadConfigSplitTests is the tripwire.

       CORRECTED 2026-09-10, SAME DAY, ROUND TWO. The sentence above said "ONLY ... four files" while
       this project was still shipping docs\compliance\*.md straight into docs\, so extracting the
       payload over an install also replaced the operator's FILLED-IN compliance pack - their signed
       sign-off log among it - with our blank templates. The claim was false when it was written, and
       it was the claim, not just the classification, that made it dangerous: the deploy script's
       preservation transcript reported "nothing changed" because docs\ was outside the folders it
       measured. The pack now ships to docs.default\ under the same rule as config.default\. The rule for
       deciding is unchanged and is stated once, above: would an operator edit it, and would their
       edit be lost?

       THE LAST EXCEPTION, CLOSED 2026-09-11 - BPScripts\. Round two of this lane claimed the property
       held "for the whole payload, not only for the config folder". The final cold gate REFUTED that:
       Pages\BestPractice.razor:87 gives the operator a "Save Script" button, :216 calls
       BPScriptService.SaveScriptContent, and Data\BPScriptService.cs:67-71 does a plain
