  <!-- == THE PAYLOAD'S PROMISE, MADE MACHINE-READABLE ===========================================
       THE INVARIANT: every file the payload is SUPPOSED to contain is ACCOUNTED FOR at run time. A
       shipped default that is promised above and is not in the payload must produce an attention entry
       from ConfigDefaultsSeeder, never silence.

       WHY IT HAD TO EXIST. A Content Update whose source file is not in the tree matches nothing,
       contributes nothing to the payload, and emits no warning of any kind - PROVED on this commit by
       Config\user-settings.json, promised a few lines up and in no build's output. The seeder could not
       notice: Data\Services\ConfigDefaultsSeeder.cs enumerates what IS in config.default\
       (Directory.GetFiles), so it cannot fail to copy a file it never looked for. A payload missing
       config.default\dashboard-config.json therefore reached Data\DashboardConfigService.cs Load,
       which generated a 3-dashboard stub where the product ships 27 - and, until this lane, PERSISTED
       it, after which the seeder Kept the stub forever.

       WHY IT IS READ AS AN XML TREE AND NOT AS TEXT. RULED 2026-09-11 (Adrian): a census that scans
       CHARACTERS is a tripwire, not a guarantee, and must say so in its own words. XmlPeek runs a real
       XPath 1.0 query over a real XML tree, so a reformat, a line wrap, a re-ordering or a change of
       attribute quoting cannot move a file out of the promised set. Two line-wrapped offenders kept
       five character-scanning censuses green on 2026-09-11; that is the defect class this avoids.

       WHY NOT MSBuild ITEM EVALUATION, which would have been shorter. Because a Content Update on an
       absent file CREATES NO ITEM - @(Content) cannot see a promise whose source is missing, which is
       exactly the trigger case. An item-evaluation census would have been vacuous for the one defect it
       exists to catch. The XML tree sees the promise whether or not the file exists.

       ABSENT OR EMPTY IS A FAILURE, NEVER A SKIP. If the XPath selects nothing, the build STOPS. A
       guard that fails toward "nothing promised" is the IP-boundary defect of 2026-09-11 all over again
       (a verifier that passed a 174 MB full build as community because it scanned a file it had not
       built, exit 0).

       THE CONSUMER is Data\Services\ShippedConfigDefaults.cs - the partial class this generates the
       other half of. The pins are Tests\SQLTriage.Tests\ShippedConfigDefaultsManifestTests.cs.
       NO _wpftmp CONDITION, DELIBERATELY. The WPF temporary-target-assembly pass compiles the whole
       project from a generated copy of this file in this same directory, so it needs the generated
       source too; $(MSBuildProjectDirectory) names this directory in both passes. -->
  <!-- ⚠ THE HOOK IS SplitResourcesByCulture - PrepareResourceNames WAS TRIED AND IS A SILENT
       NO-OP, see below - AND THE GENERATED ARTEFACT IS A RESOURCE RATHER THAN A
       SOURCE FILE, BOTH BECAUSE OF THE WPF TEMPORARY-TARGET-ASSEMBLY PASS. PROVED 2026-09-11: the
       first attempt generated a C# partial into $(IntermediateOutputPath) and added it to @(Compile).
       GenerateTemporaryTargetAssembly builds SQLTriage_<hash>_wpftmp.csproj FIRST and compiles the whole
       project from it; that pass does NOT run this target, and obj\ is outside the Compile glob, so the
       partial class had no other half and the build failed CS0103 before the real pass ever ran. Proved
       by a discriminating experiment: the generated file was put on disk by hand and the _wpftmp pass
       failed identically, so it is the Compile ITEM that is missing there, not the file. A resource is
       immune - the throwaway temp assembly does not need it, and Data\Services\ShippedConfigDefaults.cs
       compiles on its own. If the resource is ever missing from the REAL assembly the failure is loud,
       not silent: ShippedConfigDefaults.IsDerivable goes false and
       ShippedConfigDefaultsManifestTests.The_builds_promise_is_derivable_and_is_not_empty goes red.
       ⚠ THE HOOK IS MEASURED, NOT GUESSED, AND THE WRONG HOOK FAILS SILENTLY. Adding an
       EmbeddedResource after CreateManifestResourceNames has run is a NO-OP with no warning: that
       target is what stamps ManifestResourceName onto each item, and Csc is handed the result of it
       rather than @(EmbeddedResource). PROVED 2026-09-11 at -v:diag - BeforeTargets="PrepareResourceNames"
       put this target at TargetId 44, AFTER CreateManifestResourceNames at 42, and the build SUCCEEDED
       with the resource simply absent from the assembly. The only thing that caught it was
       ShippedConfigDefaultsManifestTests.The_builds_promise_is_derivable_and_is_not_empty going red,
       which is why that test exists. SplitResourcesByCulture runs BEFORE CreateManifestResourceNames,
       so the item is in the set when the names are assigned. If you move this hook, re-run that test:
       a dropped resource does not break the build, it silently empties the guard.
       ⚠ TWO LIMITS THIS TARGET DOES NOT COVER, named so the next reader does not assume it does.
       (1) The XPath matches CHILD ELEMENT metadata only. Converting ONE item to the attribute form
       TargetPath="config.default/x.json" silently shrinks the promise set; converting ALL of them trips
       the Error above. Measured by the cold gate 2026-09-12: zero attribute form metadata in the
       config.default ItemGroup today, so the promise set is complete as written.
       (2) The XPath is Condition blind, so the manifest is identical on both build profiles. That is
       CORRECT today, and measured: no Condition in the config.default ItemGroup, buildprofile.targets
       removes none of these files, and both axes ship 7. But if anyone ever profile gates one of them,
       every community operator gets a false THIS INSTALL IS INCOMPLETE at every start, because the
       promise would still name a file their payload legitimately lacks. Gate that here too, or the
       diagnostic becomes the defect. -->
  <Target Name="GenerateShippedConfigDefaultsManifest" BeforeTargets="SplitResourcesByCulture">

    <PropertyGroup>
      <_ConfigDefaultsPromiseSource>$(MSBuildProjectDirectory)\SQLTriage.csproj</_ConfigDefaultsPromiseSource>
      <_ConfigDefaultsGenerated>$(IntermediateOutputPath)shipped-config-defaults.manifest.txt</_ConfigDefaultsGenerated>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <Error Condition="!Exists('$(_ConfigDefaultsPromiseSource)')"
           Text="GenerateShippedConfigDefaultsManifest cannot read $(_ConfigDefaultsPromiseSource). The promised-defaults set is derived from that file and an empty set is a FALSE CLEAN, so this build stops." />

    <!-- The promise: every Content item whose TargetPath lands in config.default\ and that does NOT
         declare PayloadDefaultAbsentReason. -->
    <XmlPeek XmlInputPath="$(_ConfigDefaultsPromiseSource)"
             Query="//Content[starts-with(TargetPath,'config.default/')][not(PayloadDefaultAbsentReason)]/TargetPath/text()">
      <Output TaskParameter="Result" ItemName="_ConfigDefaultPromised" />
    </XmlPeek>

    <!-- The declared exceptions: promised, and the build says in its own words why the payload does not
         carry one. Accounted for, so not an attention entry - but still reported in the transcript. -->
    <XmlPeek XmlInputPath="$(_ConfigDefaultsPromiseSource)"
             Query="//Content[starts-with(TargetPath,'config.default/')][PayloadDefaultAbsentReason]/TargetPath/text()">
      <Output TaskParameter="Result" ItemName="_ConfigDefaultDeclaredAbsent" />
    </XmlPeek>

    <Error Condition="'@(_ConfigDefaultPromised)' == ''"
           Text="GenerateShippedConfigDefaultsManifest selected NO promised config.default entry from $(_ConfigDefaultsPromiseSource). Either the TargetPath spelling changed (the XPath matches 'config.default/' with a FORWARD slash) or the items moved. An empty promise set would let a missing shipped default pass unnoticed, which is the defect this target exists to catch, so this build stops rather than generating a vacuous guard." />

    <MakeDir Directories="$(IntermediateOutputPath)" />

    <WriteLinesToFile File="$(_ConfigDefaultsGenerated)" Overwrite="true" WriteOnlyWhenDifferent="true"
                      Lines="# GENERATED AT BUILD TIME by the GenerateShippedConfigDefaultsManifest target in SQLTriage.csproj. DO NOT EDIT.;# Derived by XPath 1.0 over SQLTriage.csproj read as an XML TREE. Consumer - Data/Services/ShippedConfigDefaults.cs.;@(_ConfigDefaultPromised-&gt;'promised:%(Identity)');@(_ConfigDefaultDeclaredAbsent-&gt;'declared-absent:%(Identity)')" />

    <ItemGroup>
      <EmbeddedResource Include="$(_ConfigDefaultsGenerated)">
        <LogicalName>SQLTriage.Generated.shipped-config-defaults.manifest.txt</LogicalName>
      </EmbeddedResource>
      <FileWrites Include="$(_ConfigDefaultsGenerated)" />
    </ItemGroup>
  </Target>

  <!-- ── The customer-facing updater ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
       Half two of the same ruling: the update path is a SHIPPED SCRIPT. Until now the only correct
       updater lived in tools\ (which DefaultItemExcludes keeps out of every build) and a customer had
       no way to get it, so the actual update procedure was "extract the zip over the install" — the
       exact act half one above was written to make survivable.

       It ships under a customer-facing NAME and is the SAME FILE, deliberately: tools\tests\Test-
       DeployScript.ps1 parses this script with the PowerShell AST and exercises its helper functions
       verbatim, so a forked customer copy would be an untested copy. The repo path keeps its existing
       name because Adrian runs it from there by muscle memory and a live deploy is pending on it;
       PayloadConfigSplitTests pins the mapping so the rename cannot silently drift. -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- Condition added 2026-09-20 (Adrian ruled; lane public-release): the curated public tree strips
         tools/ (.handoff/.publicignore), so publish-public.ps1 step 4b rebuilt that tree and died with
         MSB3030 on this copy. In the dev tree the file exists, so nothing about a dev build or the
         release zip changes. PayloadConfigSplitTests pins the Install-SQLTriage.ps1 mapping. -->
    <Content Include="tools\Deploy-SQLTriageService.ps1"
             Condition="Exists('tools\Deploy-SQLTriageService.ps1')"
             CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest" CopyToPublishDirectory="PreserveNewest">
      <TargetPath>Install-SQLTriage.ps1</TargetPath>
    </Content>
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <Content Include="SQLTriage.ico" CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest" />
    <!-- Canonical S-mark tile (green-on-ink, 512px) rendered into client-facing PDF reports.
         Loaded at render time from AppContext.BaseDirectory\Assets\brand\; the QuestPDF builders
         fall back to the ⛁ glyph if it is absent, so a missing asset never breaks a report. -->
    <Content Include="Assets\brand\sqltriage-mark.png" CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest" CopyToPublishDirectory="PreserveNewest" />

    <!-- ── Compliance pack (honesty-hunt strings-r1-13) ────────────────────────────────────
         Pages\AuditLogViewer.razor tells the operator to read
         docs/compliance/incident-response-runbook.md at four separate moments, and
         AuditLogService's chain-verification report body names the same file in its "Scope of
         this report" footer. That file was in the repo and in NOTHING ELSE: .md is not in the
         SDK's default Content globs, so it was never in the publish output, and
         installer/SQLTriage.iss had no docs entry at all (grep -ci docs SQLTriage.iss -> 0,
         measured 2026-08-28 before this line existed). An installed client following the banner
         found no such file - at the exact moment the app had just declared their audit chain
         broken, which is the one moment the runbook exists for.

         WHY THE WHOLE FOLDER, NOT JUST THE RUNBOOK. The runbook itself sends the reader on to
         sign-off-log.md four times (steps 7, and the three chain playbooks), and
         access-review-procedure.md and vendor-dependency-register.md each do the same. Shipping
         only the file the banner names would fix one dangling pointer by leaving four more.

         WHY UNGUARDED, like scripts\ and Assets\ in SQLTriage.iss. Nothing gates docs\ out of any
         build profile: buildprofile.targets carries no Content Remove under docs\, and
         DefaultItemExcludes above does not name it. So the folder is present in every profile and
         an unguarded rule is the loud tripwire - if the tree ever loses it, the installer compile
         aborts instead of silently shipping the dangling pointer this rule exists to close.
         DocPointerDeliveryTests carries the text tripwire for the whole class.

         WHY IT SHIPS TO docs.default\ AND NOT TO docs\ (2026-09-10, round two of lane
         customer-update-path). These four documents are OPERATOR STATE by exactly the test stated at
         the top of this file, and the first draft of this lane got that wrong: sign-off-log.md
         describes itself as a running evidence record, filled in append-only, never edited
         historically, that auditors will read; access-review-procedure.md and
         vendor-dependency-register.md ship with {{placeholder}} values the buyer replaces before
         their first audit; the runbook carries {{primary-oncall}}. Shipping them into docs\ meant
         that extracting the release over an install replaced a customer's signed compliance record
         with our blank template - measured, four files lost, the signature gone.

         So they take the config.default\ treatment: the PRISTINE templates ship here, in a folder
         nobody authors in and every route may freely overwrite, and Data\Services\ConfigDefaultsSeeder
         creates docs\compliance\<file> on first run only when it is absent. That keeps BOTH promises
         at once - a customer who deleted a document gets the current one back, a customer who filled
         one in keeps theirs, and anyone who wants to see what the current revision says reads
         docs.default\compliance\ and merges it themselves. The delivery promise the 2026-08-28 fix
         was written for is unchanged; what is gone is the destruction that paid for it.

         The TargetPath uses %(Filename)%(Extension) so the glob keeps working: a document added to
         docs\compliance\ ships as a default automatically and is seeded like the rest. -->
    <Content Include="docs\compliance\*.md" CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest" CopyToPublishDirectory="PreserveNewest">
      <TargetPath>docs.default\compliance\%(Filename)%(Extension)</TargetPath>
    </Content>
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- ── Export Pack identity manifest (E3 verify MUST-FIX #1) ─────────────────────────────
       identity_manifest.sql is what the export lane runs against the selected instance to get
       the instance's OWN declaration of every real name it knows, so the tokenizer replaces
       from an authoritative list instead of guessing. Until this line it lived ONLY in the
       sibling private meta checkout, resolved through MetaRepoPath / SQLTRIAGE_META_REPO —
       so on a normal install (and on a client's production box, which has no such checkout)
       every Run refused. Same reason R1b moved the canonical kernel into the app: a client
       box cannot be asked to hold a private repo.

       The SOURCE sits under Data\Services\Portal\Export\ so the existing portal denies cover
       it (.handoff\.publicignore Data/Services/Portal/), and the Include is conditioned on
       the SAME property that Compile-Removes that whole tree — a community build carries
       neither the code that reads it nor the file. Condition is written here rather than as a
       Content Remove in buildprofile.targets because that file is imported at the TOP of this
       one, so a Remove there cannot see an Include added below it.

       It ships as scripts\identity_manifest.sql, beside the other SQL the app runs
       (sp_Blitz.sql, SQLDBA.ORG.sp_triage.sql). Read-only; it changes no state.

       The Exists() guard is the same one Config\free-bundle.dat carries: the public mirror is
       published WITHOUT this tree (.publicignore), and a literal Content Include of a file that
       is not there fails the copy step rather than evaluating away. A build that needs the file
       and lacks it is caught by ExportPackCompositionTests, not by an MSBuild error. -->
  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(SQLTExcludePortal)' != 'true' And Exists('Data\Services\Portal\Export\identity_manifest.sql')">
    <None Remove="Data\Services\Portal\Export\identity_manifest.sql" />
    <Content Include="Data\Services\Portal\Export\identity_manifest.sql">
      <CopyToOutputDirectory>PreserveNewest</CopyToOutputDirectory>
      <CopyToPublishDirectory>PreserveNewest</CopyToPublishDirectory>
      <TargetPath>scripts\identity_manifest.sql</TargetPath>
    </Content>
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- Phase 3: BIP39 wordlist for license key encoding/decoding.
       Run tools\fetch-bip39-wordlist.ps1 once to download the wordlist file.
       The Condition prevents the build from failing if the file has not been fetched yet. -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <EmbeddedResource Include="Resources\bip39-english.txt" Condition="Exists('Resources\bip39-english.txt')" />
    <!-- Trusted public key for verifying signed update artifacts. Until the real
         code-signing cert is dropped in (tools\extract-update-pubkey.ps1), this is a
         placeholder and UpdateSignatureVerifier fails closed. Public key only — never
         the private key. -->
    <EmbeddedResource Include="Resources\update-signing-public.pem" Condition="Exists('Resources\update-signing-public.pem')" />
    <!-- S3: Ola Hallengren's Maintenance Solution (MIT-licensed), embedded so
         MaintenanceSolutionOpRenderer can load + checksum-verify it at runtime without
         depending on the loose BPScripts\ copy surviving deployment. BPScripts is excluded
         from default globbing (DefaultItemExcludes above does not list it, but it also has
         no implicit EmbeddedResource glob) — Include it explicitly. The Exists() guard is the same
         one its siblings above carry: the public mirror is published WITHOUT BPScripts
         (.publicignore), and a literal Include of a file that is not there fails the build rather
         than evaluating away. -->
    <EmbeddedResource Include="BPScripts\01. MaintenanceSolution.sql" Condition="Exists('BPScripts\01. MaintenanceSolution.sql')" />
    <!-- Ruling 2026-08-23 #4: the shipped script-configuration defaults, embedded so an install
         UPGRADED by Inno (which ships Config\script-configurations.json with onlyifdoesntexist
         and so keeps the operator's older file) still has the shipped entries available at
         startup for ScriptConfigurationMigrator to add the missing ones. Embedded rather than
         shipped as a second loose file because the installer then cannot get it wrong, and
         because the same Config\ file is ALSO Content (copied to config/ at build) - so both
         copies come from one source, and ScriptConfigurationMigratorTests byte-compares them to
         keep it that way. Not profile-gated: this file is Content in every profile. -->
    <EmbeddedResource Include="Config\script-configurations.json" />
    <!-- Same shape, same remedy: Config\alert-definitions.json is preserved on upgrade by the
         installer (onlyifdoesntexist), by AutoUpdateService.ProtectedConfigFiles, by
         SQLTriageUpdater's ProtectedConfig and by the service deploy script, so a catalogue
         property added after an install ships never reaches it. valueKind is one such property
         and it is the only switch that stops six alerts comparing a since-startup counter total
         to a per-second threshold, so an upgraded install would have kept firing the very false
         Criticals the fix exists to stop. Embedded so AlertDefinitionMigrator has the shipped
         catalogue without depending on the installer delivering a second loose copy; the same
         Config\ file is ALSO Content (copied to config/ at build), and
         AlertCumulativeRateTests byte-compares the two to keep them one source. Not
         profile-gated: this file is Content in every profile. -->
    <EmbeddedResource Include="Config\alert-definitions.json" />
    <!-- Same shape, same remedy: Config\dashboard-config.json is preserved on upgrade by the installer
         (onlyifdoesntexist), by AutoUpdateService.ProtectedConfigFiles, by SQLTriageUpdater's
         ProtectedConfig and by the service deploy script, so a panel this product shipped broken and
         has since corrected never reaches an existing install — the only prior merge path,
         PatchMissingDashboards, adds whole DASHBOARDS absent by id (all three generated ones already
         present) and touches no panel. Embedded so DashboardConfigMigrator has the corrected shipped
         bodies without depending on the installer delivering a second loose copy; the same Config\
         file is ALSO Content (copied to config/ at build). Not profile-gated: this file is Content in
         every profile. -->
    <EmbeddedResource Include="Config\dashboard-config.json" />
    <!-- The pre-fix panel bodies the dashboards lane superseded, the match table
         DashboardConfigMigrator re-bases against. Embedded only; Content-Removed above so it never
         ships to an install's config\ folder. -->
    <EmbeddedResource Include="Config\dashboard-config.superseded.json" />
