  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- ── BPScripts.default\ : OPERATOR-EDITABLE, seeded when absent, refreshed only when stock ──
         2026-09-11, lane bpscripts-operator-edits-are-lost. THE INVARIANT: no folder the application
         writes to at runtime may sit on the update overwrite path. Pages\BestPractice.razor:87 gives the
         operator a Save Script button and Data\BPScriptService.cs:67-71 writes it straight into
         <install>\BPScripts, so that folder is operator state - and it sat in the deploy script's
         $copyItems and in neither $preservedFolders nor $deliberatelyNotCopied.

         THE FIX IS ON THE PAYLOAD, NOT ON A COPY LIST, and that is the whole point. The applier every
         shipped install actually runs is Data\AutoUpdateService.cs:740 -
         robocopy %SRC% "<appDir>" /E /IS /IT - wholesale, with no /XF and no /XD. A list-driven
         exclusion cannot reach it, and the by-list updater that could is unshipped
         (AutoUpdateService.cs:575-582 says so in terms). So the stock scripts stop shipping as
         BPScripts\ altogether. The SOURCE folder keeps its name - the embedded MaintenanceSolution
         resource below and the community profile's BPScripts\** removals both address the source -
         and only the PAYLOAD path moves. Then robocopy has nothing to clobber with, the deploy script
         has nothing to copy, the installer has nothing to overwrite, and
         ConfigDefaultsSeeder.RunBPScripts creates <install>\BPScripts, per file, when a file is
         absent. One change, every route.

         AND IT REFRESHES A FILE IT CAN PROVE IS OURS, ruled by Adrian 2026-09-11 after the cold gate
         measured what seed-once alone does to installs that already exist. All eleven stock scripts
         are already present on one, so create-if-absent Kept every one of them - freezing EVERY
         install at its installed revision, not only the ones somebody had edited. Improvements do
         reach installs today: that folder's Install-All-Scripts.sql is dated 26 Aug against 10 Jul for
         the other ten, carried in by the wholesale robocopy. So the pass now hashes each existing file
         and compares it against ShippedBPScriptRevisions, a manifest of every revision of every stock
         script this product has ever shipped, recovered from git history. Bytes we shipped are
         refreshed to the current default; anything else is the operator's and is kept. A single
         comparison against the CURRENT default cannot do this - on an existing install "differs from
         the current default" means EITHER an operator edit OR a newer release, and those are not
         distinguishable without the history.

         CONSEQUENCE, STATED NOT DISCOVERED: an operator who has EDITED a stock script still never
         receives an improved version of that script again - their bytes are not in the manifest, so it
         is kept. That is the trade Adrian ruled for on 2026-09-10 (DECISIONS, widget: SEED ONCE, keep
         the button) and 2026-09-11 does not revisit it. The current
         revision stays readable in BPScripts.default\ and they merge it themselves. And a customer
         whose scripts were ALREADY destroyed does not get them back; this stops the next loss only.
         FAIL-SAFE DIRECTION: a file whose hash is unknown - including one predating the manifest - is
         treated as operator-modified and KEPT, so the failure mode is a missed improvement rather than
         a destroyed edit. ShippedBPScriptRevisionsTests is the anti-rot pin: it derives the file set
         from the TargetPath elements below and goes red if a revision ships unrecorded.
         RuntimeWriteFolderCensusTests is the standing pin, derived from the code that writes;
         BPScriptServiceTests exercises the seeding rule and the robocopy shape. -->
    <None Remove="BPScripts\\Ignore\\*\\*;SQLTriage-RAG-Builder\\*\\***" />
    <Content Remove="BPScripts\\Ignore\\*\\*;SQLTriage-RAG-Builder\\*\\***" />
    <!-- ⚠ THE PAYLOAD MUST CONTAIN NO BPScripts\ FOLDER AT ALL, AND WITHOUT THIS LINE IT STILL DID.
         MEASURED 2026-09-11 on a full Debug publish AFTER the retarget above: the payload held
         BPScripts.default\ with all eleven scripts AND a second folder, BPScripts\ruleset.json,
         771 KB. No rule in this file asks for that file. It arrives through the Web SDK's IMPLICIT
         Content globs - **\*.config and **\*.json carry CopyToPublishDirectory=PreserveNewest - so
         any .json left in the source BPScripts\ folder publishes into a payload BPScripts\ folder,
         and the wholesale robocopy at Data\AutoUpdateService.cs:740 (/E /IS /IT, no /XF, no /XD)
         then creates <install>\BPScripts and writes into it. That is the precise property this lane
         exists to remove, surviving the retarget by a route no copy list can see.

         NOTHING READS THE FILE: Data\Services\SqlAssessmentService.cs:71 loads
         Config/ruleset.json out of the licence BUNDLE, never off disk, and no production source
         resolves BPScripts\ruleset.json. So this removes 771 KB of dead payload as well.

         NO OPERATOR FILE WAS EVER AT RISK FROM IT, and that is worth stating because it is why this
